<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

CAT-SOOP pages are rendered on the server for each request.  The page source is
not sent directly to the browser: CAT-SOOP combines configuration, user
information, request values, source markup, and questions to produce the final
HTML response.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>The Rendering Pipeline</catsoop-section>

For a normal course page, CAT-SOOP:

1. resolves the URL to a directory `content.*` file or a single-file page;
2. gathers URL query arguments and POST fields into `cs_form`;
3. runs inherited `preload.py` files from the course root down to the page;
4. authenticates the visitor and loads their course user information;
5. loads the page source and expands `<include>` tags;
6. runs `<python>` blocks and `\@{...}` expressions;
7. converts Markdown when the source is `.catsoop` or `.md`;
8. creates the questions described by `<question>` blocks;
9. runs the selected page handler and wraps the result in the site template.

This means page Python can use values from `preload.py`, `cs_user_info`, and
`cs_form`.  It also means changing request-dependent Python can change the
questions or prose produced for that request, so question names and grading
behavior should remain stable.

<catsoop-section>Mixing Markdown and HTML</catsoop-section>

`.catsoop` and `.md` pages accept ordinary Markdown and raw HTML in the same
file:

```html
## A Markdown heading

This is a Markdown paragraph with **emphasis**.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-info">
<b>This portion is HTML.</b><br>
It can use CSS classes and elements that Markdown does not express directly.
</div>
```

Use Markdown for ordinary prose, lists, links, and code blocks.  Use HTML for
layout or components that genuinely need elements, attributes, or course CSS.
Keeping most prose in Markdown generally makes the source easier to read and
maintain.

<catsoop-subsection>The Blank-Line Gotcha</catsoop-subsection>

Markdown treats a run of raw HTML as an HTML block.  A blank line inside that
block can end the raw-HTML portion and cause the following lines to be parsed
as Markdown.  The result may contain unexpected `<p>` elements or a layout
different from what the source indentation suggests.

For predictable raw HTML, do not put empty lines inside one HTML block:

```html
<div class="notice">
<p>First paragraph.</p>
<p>Second paragraph.</p>
</div>
```

This is less predictable because of the empty line:

```html
<div class="notice">
First paragraph.

Second paragraph.
</div>
```

If the contents should be Markdown, put a blank line immediately after an
opening `<div>`:

```html
<div class="notice">

**Important:** this content is intentionally parsed as Markdown.

</div>
```

CAT-SOOP recognizes that form and enables Markdown processing inside the
`<div>`.  Whichever mode you intend, be deliberate: compact raw HTML contains
no empty lines, while a Markdown-containing `<div>` starts with one.

<catsoop-section>Request Values in cs_form</catsoop-section>

`cs_form` is a dictionary available to page and handler Python.  It combines:

* arguments from the URL query string, such as `?view=summary`;
* fields from a POST request, such as an HTML form submission or an AJAX call.

For a request such as:

```text
/numerical_methods/status?view=summary
```

page code can read:

```python
view = cs_form.get("view", "summary")
```

If a POST request also sends `student=ada`, the same request context exposes:

```python
cs_form["view"]       # "summary", from the URL
cs_form["student"]    # "ada", from the POST body
```

Values are normally strings.  Uploaded files may be represented as bytes, and
when the same field is repeated CAT-SOOP keeps one value, so do not use repeated
keys as a list protocol.  Avoid using the same key in both the query string and
POST body; the collision is ambiguous to course code.

`cs_qstring` is also available when code specifically needs the parsed query
string rather than the combined form.  Its values are lists, following
`urllib.parse.parse_qs`.

<catsoop-subsection>Using Request Data Safely</catsoop-subsection>

Everything in `cs_form` is supplied by a browser or API client and is
untrusted.  Validate types and allowed values before using it:

```python
allowed_views = {"summary", "full"}
view = cs_form.get("view", "summary")
if view not in allowed_views:
    view = "summary"
```

Escape a request value before inserting it into HTML:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;python&gt;
import html

label = html.escape(cs_form.get("label", "Untitled"))
print("&lt;p&gt;%s&lt;/p&gt;" % label)
&lt;/python&gt;
</code></pre>

Do not pass `cs_form` values to `eval`, `exec`, a shell command, or a file path
without strict validation.  Question-answer POSTs are handled by the qtype and
page handler; ordinary page code should not attempt to reproduce CAT-SOOP's
grading protocol.
